You are reproducing a Python terminal PDF viewer from scratch.
Write one complete, self-contained Python 3 script that satisfies the
specification below.
Output only the script's source as your final deliverable, with no commentary
beyond what is needed to deliver it. **Purpose:** A terminal-based PDF viewer
that renders PDF pages as images using half-block characters and ANSI escape
sequences, with keyboard navigation, zoom, and page jumping.
It is intended for Termux on Android but should run on any POSIX terminal.
**Shebang and header:** Start with a Termux-style env shebang
(`/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`) and `from __future__ import
annotations`. **Dependencies:** - Standard library only, plus PyMuPDF.
Import `fitz`; if that fails, fall back to `pymupdf` imported as `fitz`; if both
fail, call `sys.exit` with the message "PyMuPDF is required: pip install
pymupdf". - Standard modules used: `argparse`, `math`, `os`, `select`, `shutil`,
`sys`, `termios`, `tty`.
The terminal control relies on POSIX-only `termios` and `tty`. **Constants:**
Define ANSI sequences as module-level strings: reset, cursor home, clear screen,
hide/show cursor, enter/leave alternate screen, reverse video, and the upper
half-block character (U+2580). **`read_key(fd, timeout=None)` function:** - Use
`select` to wait on the file descriptor with an optional timeout.
Return `None` if nothing is ready. - Read one byte.
If empty (EOF), return `"q"`. - If the byte is not ESC (`0x1b`), return it
decoded as UTF-8 with replacement. - If it is ESC, collect an escape sequence:
keep reading single bytes while more are available within 30 ms, up to 8 bytes
total, stopping after an alphabetic byte or `~`.
Return the sequence decoded as latin-1.
This lets arrow keys and similar be recognized. **`Viewer` class:** - Class
constants: minimum zoom 0.25, maximum zoom 8.0, zoom step 1.25, page render
cache limit 6 entries. - Constructor `(path, page=1, zoom=1.0)`: open the
document with `fitz.open`.
If the page count is zero, raise an error with a clear message.
Store the path, document, starting page (1-based, clamped into range), and zoom
(clamped to min/max). - Maintain an LRU-style page render cache keyed by page
and zoom/size, evicting the oldest beyond the limit. - Rendering: render each
page to a pixmap with PyMuPDF scaled to the terminal's available cell grid.
Each terminal cell row displays two pixel rows using the half-block character,
with foreground color for the top pixel and background color for the bottom
pixel, using 24-bit ANSI color escapes.
Fit the page to terminal width/height with the zoom factor applied, preserving
aspect ratio, and center it. - Show a status line at the bottom (or top) with
file name, current page number over total, zoom percentage, and a short key-help
hint, rendered in reverse video.
Truncate to the terminal width using `shutil.get_terminal_size`. - Handle
terminal resize by re-querying size each frame and invalidating cached renders
when dimensions change. **Controls (handled via `read_key`):** - `q` quits.
End of input also quits. - Next page: `j`, down arrow, space, `n`, PageDown.
Previous page: `k`, up arrow, `p`, PageUp.
Clamp at first and last pages; do not wrap. - `g` / Home goes to first page; `G`
/ End goes to last page. - Zoom in with `+` or `=`, zoom out with `-` or `_`,
multiplied/divided by the zoom step and clamped; `0` resets zoom to 1.0. -
Go-to-page prompt via `:` or `g`-style numeric entry: accept digits, Enter
confirms, Escape or Backspace-to-empty cancels, out-of-range values clamp. -
Ignore unrecognized keys. **Terminal handling:** - Save the terminal attributes
with `termios.tcgetattr` on stdin, switch to cbreak/raw mode with
`tty.setcbreak` or `tty.setraw`, and enter the alternate screen, hide the
cursor, and clear on entry. - On exit (including exceptions and
KeyboardInterrupt), restore the original terminal attributes via
`termios.tcsetattr`, show the cursor, leave the alternate screen, and reset
colors.
Use try/finally. - Write output to stdout with a single buffered write per
frame, flushing afterward, to avoid flicker. **`main()` / CLI:** - Use
`argparse` with a required positional `pdf` path, an optional `-p/--page`
integer (1-based, default 1), and an optional `-z/--zoom` float (default 1.0). -
Verify the file exists and is readable; on failure print an error to stderr and
exit with a nonzero code. - If stdin is not a TTY, print an error to stderr and
exit nonzero, since interactive mode requires a terminal. - Catch `fitz` open
errors and report them to stderr with a nonzero exit. - Guard the entry point
with `if __name__ == "__main__":` and call `sys.exit(main())`. **Edge cases to
handle:** empty PDFs, pages with zero size, very small terminals (render
degenerate or minimum size without crashing), extreme zoom levels clamped,
invalid or out-of-range page arguments clamped, non-ASCII file names, and
partial escape sequences read by `read_key`. **Style:** Use type hints
throughout, concise docstrings or comments only where helpful, and clear helper
methods separating rendering, input handling, and terminal setup. ---
